<p>It's October. New month, new <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell">agent-shell</a> update. If you missed the last post, have a look at the <a href="https://xenodium.com/agent-shell-0-78-updates">0.78 update</a>. While the latest changes are focused primarily on bugfixes and polish, we have a handful of new features too.</p>
<p>As usual, this post showcases highlights, but please check out the <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/compare/v0.78.2...v0.87.4">full list of changes</a> if you're after the nitty-gritty.</p>
<h2>What's <code>agent-shell</code>?</h2>
<p><a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell">agent-shell</a> brings your favorite AI agents to <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/">Emacs</a>, keeping you at home: familiar buffers and bindings, no terminal emulation. All powered by ACP, the <a href="https://agentclientprotocol.com/">Agent Client Protocol</a>.</p>
<h2>Happy birthday <code>agent-shell</code></h2>
<p>I <a href="https://xenodium.com/introducing-agent-shell">introduced <code>agent-shell</code></a> just over a year ago, with support for two agents: Claude and Gemini. Today, it speaks to 23.</p>
<p>Development hasn't let up either. Improvements and new features land every month.</p>
<p>Thank you to all users, contributors, and sponsors.</p>
<h2>New supported agents</h2>
<p>A couple more join the family, bringing it to <strong>23 agents</strong>. As usual, they're all reachable via <code>M-x agent-shell</code>, or go straight to the new ones:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://junie.jetbrains.com/docs/junie-cli-acp.html">Junie</a> (JetBrains), <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/847">#847</a> by <a href="https://github.com/thanhvg">@thanhvg</a>. Start it with <code>M-x agent-shell-junie-start-agent</code>.</li>
<li><a href="https://openclaw.ai">OpenClaw</a>, <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/878">#878</a> by <a href="https://github.com/jtmoulia">@jtmoulia</a>. Start it with <code>M-x agent-shell-openclaw-start-agent</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p><img src="https://xenodium.github.io/images/agent-shell-0-87-updates/agents.png" alt=""></p>
<h2>Animated prompts</h2>
<p>With the ability to prompt at any time (<a href="https://xenodium.com/agent-shell-0-78-updates#prompt-any-time">see my last post</a>), you can queue prompts or steer agents straight from the persistent shell prompt. Animating the prompt ties it all together: it's now obvious at a glance that the agent is busy, and that anything else you submit will queue.</p>
<p>Check out <code>agent-shell-prompt-busy-frames</code> for relevant customizations (more on that shortly).</p>
<p><img src="https://xenodium.github.io/images/agent-shell-0-87-updates/animated-prompt.gif" alt=""></p>
<p>Thanks to <a href="https://github.com/cark">@cark</a> for the <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/843">proposal</a>.</p>
<h2>Busy indicators get further customizations</h2>
<p>The header's busy indicator has been animated for some time now. Amongst the existing customizations, <code>agent-shell-show-busy-indicator</code> could turn it off altogether. Having said that, there's now a middle ground: use static text via <code>agent-shell-busy-indicator-frames</code>.</p>
<p>Just set it to a string like so:</p>
<pre><code class="language-{.commonlisp">(setopt agent-shell-busy-indicator-frames &quot;(busy)&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>If you'd like to use smarter logic, you can now set it to a function as follows:</p>
<pre><code class="language-{.commonlisp">(setopt agent-shell-busy-indicator-frames
        (lambda ()
          (if (agent-shell-session-id)
              &quot;(busy)&quot;
            &quot;(connecting)&quot;)))
</code></pre>
<p><code>agent-shell-prompt-busy-frames</code> takes all the same forms, so the prompt's own animation can also be a list of frames, a static string, or a function.</p>
<h2>Queue merging</h2>
<p>With the new <code>agent-shell-prompt-queue-merge</code> setting (enabled by default), pending prompts are joined (separated by blank lines) and submitted as a single turn. Queued prompts are typically follow-ups to the same instruction, so the agent now sees them together rather than acting on the first before reading the rest.</p>
<p>If you prefer the previous behaviour, disable it with:</p>
<pre><code class="language-{.commonlisp">(setopt agent-shell-prompt-queue-merge nil)
</code></pre>
<p>Thank you <a href="https://github.com/Gleek">@Gleek</a> for the <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/791">proposal</a>.</p>
<p>While on the subject of queueing, cancelling now offers to continue with the queue rather than quietly dropping it, and killing a shell now warns you about the pending work you'd lose.</p>
<h2>More idiomatic completion</h2>
<p><code>@</code> and <code>/</code> completion used to bundle two things: how to complete, and when to pop up. Turning the popping off lost completion altogether, leaving <a href="https://github.com/minad/corfu">corfu</a> and company users out.</p>
<p>They're now decoupled. The completion functions are always registered, so <code>completion-at-point</code>, corfu and company work either way. Two options decide whether completion pops as you type: <code>agent-shell-file-completion-enabled</code> for <code>@</code> and <code>agent-shell-command-completion-enabled</code> for <code>/</code>. For example, listing files can be slow in a large project, so to only complete <code>@</code> on demand while <code>/</code> keeps popping:</p>
<pre><code class="language-{.commonlisp">(setopt agent-shell-file-completion-enabled nil)
</code></pre>
<p>Trailing spaces (and <code>@</code> mention quoting) are now only inserted on a finished completion, fixing the stray spaces corfu and company users were seeing.</p>
<p>Thanks to <a href="https://github.com/bcc32">@bcc32</a> for proposing the split and separate <code>@</code> and <code>/</code> options in <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/537">#537</a>, and to <a href="https://github.com/jcubic">@jcubic</a> for filing it and flagging the stray spaces.</p>
<h2>Moving by blocks</h2>
<p><code>C-M-a</code> and <code>C-M-e</code> (<code>beginning-of-defun</code> and <code>end-of-defun</code>) now move between blocks. Thank you <a href="https://github.com/OSadovy">@OSadovy</a> for <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/876">#876</a>.</p>
<h2>Customizable chat labels</h2>
<p>Chat mode draws &quot;Me&quot; and &quot;Agent&quot; labels (or rather &quot;Claude&quot;, &quot;Codex&quot; etc.). If the current style isn't your jam, <code>agent-shell-chat-label-function</code> now lets you draw them yourself. It's handed an alist with <code>:text</code> and <code>:role</code> (either <code>user</code> or <code>agent</code>), and must return a single-line propertized string.</p>
<p>The <code>display</code> property is honored, so labels can also be rendered as images. Thanks to <a href="https://github.com/nhojb">@nhojb</a> for the idea.</p>
<h2>Parseable transcripts</h2>
<p>Transcripts now open with YAML frontmatter, including a start timestamp as follows:</p>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">---
agent: &quot;Claude&quot;
started: &quot;2026-10-11T09:30:12+01:00&quot;
working_directory: &quot;~/stuff/active/code/agent-shell&quot;
session_id: &quot;0f3c9a1e-5b7d-4e2a-9c81-6d4f2b8e7a35&quot;
model: &quot;claude-opus-4-5&quot;
---
</code></pre>
<p>Thank you <a href="https://github.com/vermiculus">@vermiculus</a> for <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/854">#854</a>.</p>
<p>While on topic, transcripts also got a little sturdier, thanks to <a href="https://github.com/mrcnski">@mrcnski</a>'s <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/859">proposal</a>.</p>
<h2><code>M-x yank-media</code> support</h2>
<p><code>M-x yank-media</code> now pastes images straight from the system clipboard (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/853">#853</a>), no external utilities needed, the same way Org mode handles it. Pasted image previews also survive being queued or steered, thanks to <a href="https://github.com/Gleek">@Gleek</a> for <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/852">#852</a>.</p>
<h2>Codex config options</h2>
<p>Codex gained <code>agent-shell-openai-default-config-options</code> for setting config options at session start, reasoning effort included. For example:</p>
<pre><code class="language-{.commonlisp">(setopt agent-shell-openai-default-config-options
        '((&quot;reasoning_effort&quot; . &quot;high&quot;)))
</code></pre>
<p>Thank you <a href="https://github.com/ianthetechie">@ianthetechie</a> for <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/855">#855</a>.</p>
<h2>Tool call output</h2>
<p>Tool call output can be fairly long. A single chunky line was enough to bog a buffer down. Long tool output lines are now limited (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/844">#844</a> by <a href="https://github.com/Gleek">@Gleek</a>), and fallback titles are truncated so they no longer flash chunkily as they stream.</p>
<p>File summaries also count distinct files now, so reading the same file three times counts as one file rather than three (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/863">#863</a>, also by <a href="https://github.com/Gleek">@Gleek</a>).</p>
<h2>A bunch of fixes</h2>
<p>While we have a handful of new features, the bulk of this release went into fixes.</p>
<p>Rendering:</p>
<ul>
<li>Emphasis no longer swallows asterisks inside inline code (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/870">#870</a>).</li>
<li>Bold around inline code now renders, as does a trailing quote or header line.</li>
<li>Streamed tables keep their alignment and markup (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/868">#868</a>).</li>
<li><code>M-x agent-shell-copy-as-markdown</code> no longer mangles bullets carrying bold text (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/867">#867</a>).</li>
<li>Expanding or collapsing an item inside a group now works mid-stream.</li>
<li>Chat mode no longer hides notices rendered above the live prompt.</li>
<li>The graphical header no longer gets cut off when showing cost (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/849">#849</a>).</li>
<li>Terminal hyperlinks (osc-8) are rendered (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/850">#850</a> by <a href="https://github.com/gnufied">@gnufied</a>).</li>
</ul>
<p>Performance and responsiveness:</p>
<ul>
<li>Severe cursor lag from large collapsed tool outputs is gone (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/839">#839</a>).</li>
<li>Bare <code>path:line</code> references are now found in linear time (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/848">#848</a> by <a href="https://github.com/catern">@catern</a>), and failed searches leave point where it was.</li>
<li>Heartbeat ticks skip redrawing when the busy frames haven't changed.</li>
<li>In <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/acp.el">acp.el</a>, draining the agent's output is now bounded in how long it holds the main thread (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/acp.el/pull/32">#32</a> by <a href="https://github.com/moyotar">@moyotar</a>), so a chatty agent no longer makes Emacs feel laggy.</li>
<li>In <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/shell-maker">shell-maker</a>, launching a shell with <code>visual-wrap-prefix-mode</code> enabled no longer hangs Emacs (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/842">#842</a>).</li>
</ul>
<p>Elsewhere:</p>
<ul>
<li>Forking resumes the session, and keeps the fork rather than falling back to another session if that resume fails (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/871">#871</a>).</li>
<li>Grouped config options are handled (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/851">#851</a>), and a missing config change event is now emitted (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/864">#864</a>).</li>
<li><code>display-buffer-overriding-action</code> is preserved when displaying asynchronously (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/846">#846</a> by <a href="https://github.com/bcc32">@bcc32</a>), and docstring command binding specifiers are fixed (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/845">#845</a>, also by <a href="https://github.com/bcc32">@bcc32</a>).</li>
<li>Shell command output now runs in the shell's directory via its file handler (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/pull/879">#879</a> by <a href="https://github.com/junyi-hou">@junyi-hou</a>), which matters for TRAMP users.</li>
<li>The README gained guidance for agents hanging at &quot;Loading…&quot; over TRAMP (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/872">#872</a>), which turned out to be about <code>tramp-remote-path</code> and direct async processes rather than a bug.</li>
</ul>
<h2>News on the welcome screen</h2>
<p>New shells now show the last few release posts on the welcome screen, so they are a little more discoverable.</p>
<p><img src="https://xenodium.github.io/images/agent-shell-0-87-updates/news.png" alt=""></p>
<h2>Public functions</h2>
<p>A few additions for package authors building on <code>agent-shell</code>.</p>
<h3>Knowing when a shell is ready</h3>
<p><code>agent-shell-initialized-p</code> returns non-nil once the handshake, authentication, session and any default options are all done. Until then, subscribe to the new <code>init-finished</code> event via <code>agent-shell-subscribe-to</code> (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/860">#860</a>).</p>
<h3>Reading and setting config options</h3>
<p>Agent config options are now reachable from elisp rather than the UI alone:</p>
<pre><code class="language-{.commonlisp">(agent-shell-config-options)
(agent-shell-config-option :category &quot;thought_level&quot;)
(agent-shell-config-option-value :id &quot;effort&quot;)
(agent-shell-set-config-option-value :id &quot;effort&quot; :value &quot;high&quot;
                                     :on-success (lambda (_) (message &quot;Set&quot;)))
</code></pre>
<h3>Other bits</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>agent-shell-last-activity-time</code> returns when the shell last saw a submitted prompt or an incoming notification (<a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell/issues/856">#856</a>), handy for idle tracking.</li>
<li><code>M-x agent-shell-save-traffic</code> writes the session's ACP traffic to a <code>.traffic</code> file and reveals it in <code>dired</code>, simplifying the bug-filing process.</li>
</ul>
<h2>New third-party package</h2>
<p><a href="https://github.com/izeigerman/agent-shell-gc">agent-shell-gc</a> joins the lot, bringing buffer garbage collection (auto-kill) to <code>agent-shell</code>.</p>
